(in-progress:)
  * A menu's price column lines up when an item name overruns the column
  * An inter-BBS turn opens with the BBS Coordinator's name, or the realm the
    current vote is for
  * The Queen's lottery is offered once a game day, after her tax refund: a
    six-letter ticket costing 5,000 gold, paying 2,500 to 10,000,000 by letters
    matched
  * New bbs.cfg setting "Lottery": no turns the lottery off for this board
  * bbs.cfg is read even when config.json is absent, where its settings used to
    be ignored entirely on a board that had not saved a config yet
  * A routed league's broadcasts are signed after they are addressed, where
    every copy used to be refused by the boards that check keys
  * A packet refused for not matching the sender's key is reported as refused,
    where it was counted and announced as applied
  * The captured-region picker lists the Advisors entry and takes * to visit
    them, as the Buy Regions screen does — the original draws both screens from
    one routine
  * bbs.cfg is read and never written by the game: -ibbs-reset prints it for the
    sysop to save, where a rules reset used to return the board's name, league
    number and packet directories to their defaults (issue #152)
  * The settings editor shows those four settings but no longer changes them,
    naming bbs.cfg as where they are edited
  * New -league-check command line option: it reports the roster, the board
    name, the packet directories and the keys in one pass (issue #154)
  * Joining a league with a board name that is not in the roster is refused at
    setup, naming the closest entry (issue #154)
  * barons-ftn's thin-margin warning wraps to the console width, and drops
    advice that no longer applies (issue #156)
  * A bid on an allied planet's listing priced at 0 gold is accepted. It was
    refused with a message about the quantity
  * New -players command line option, the original's VIEW: lists the players
    and changes a player's name, renames a realm, or deletes one (issue #161)
  * The ChangeLog ships in the release and snapshot archives
  * Send SpyGuy now works as the original's does: a watcher posted on a planet
    for a number of days, paid for in gold by the day rather than with an agent,
    who reports group attacks and Clingy Annihilators aimed at your planet into
    your planet news. He cannot be caught, and the planet he watches is never
    told he is there
  * A Clingy Annihilator under construction is no longer announced to the planet
    it is aimed at. That planet learns of it from a SpyGuy, or when the weapon
    launches
  * The Spy Database is filled by interplanetary covert operations reporting on
    the realms they strike, as in the original, rather than by a scouting errand
    of its own
  * New help topic: Send SpyGuy
  * Messages arriving from another planet no longer appear in the planet news.
    They are delivered to the mailboxes they are addressed to and nowhere else
    (issue #146)
  * The BBS Coordinator can stand down the planet's Clingy Annihilator before it
    flies, from the Coordinator menu, whoever built it (issue #45). The baron
    who started it keeps their own dismantle for now (issue #114)
  * The Coordinator's Player List no longer shows the BBS account name behind
    each realm. The original prints one only in a file a sysop creates, never
    on a screen
  * The ballot for BBS Coordinator lists every realm again. Realms under
    new-realm protection were left off it, so early in a game a voter was the
    only candidate on their own ballot (issue #149)
  * Coordinator menu items moved onto the original's keys: Modify Diplomacy 2,
    Global Recon Request 3, View Diplomacy 4, Player List 5. Key 1 is held for
    Dismantle Gooie (issue #114)
  * An empty granary no longer stops a realm's population from growing. A realm
    that fed its people in full at the maintenance prompts lost that turn's
    growth for having spent its food doing so
  * End of Turn Statistics now reports a turn with no population movement as
    "gained 0", matching the original; the line was omitted entirely, so a realm
    out of food was told nothing
  * Onboarding messages wrap to the screen instead of splitting words at column 80
  * A realm name may now be made entirely of CP437 glyphs (issue #151)
  * An idle timeout while naming a realm now ends the session, creating none
  * A realm may be renamed once, from the Preferences menu, after protection ends
  * CP437 characters typed at a prompt now arrive as typed (issue #151)
  * -planetary now reports skipped packets by reason (wrong league, mesh copy,
    already seen) instead of silently discarding them (issue #139)
  * New -full, -scores, and -detailed flags for sysop batch files, matching BRE's
    command-line arguments (issue #148)
  * Extra blank lines removed from maintenance payment prompts; each "How much
    will you give?" now sits on the same line as its requirement, matching BRE
  * A realm now holds one of the planet's 25 seats for its whole life, and its
    letter is that seat. The letter no longer moves when another realm joins or
    falls, and the Id column on See Scores shows it, so those rows are no longer
    in letter order
  * A planet holds 25 realms and no more. A caller arriving when every seat is
    taken is told so and given no realm. A realm past the 25th used to be
    created in silence, able to act on everyone while appearing in nobody's
    target list
  * Computer barons take seats from the 25, so a board running them seats fewer
    callers. Max Players Per BBS set to 0 now means "as many as the seats left"
    rather than "unlimited"
  * A seat freed when a realm is swept from the map is given to the next realm
    founded, which inherits none of its predecessor's pacts, mail or market
    stock
  * A realm under New Realm Protection is refused the covert operations that
    have an effect, and the interplanetary Send SpyGuy, at no cost in gold,
    agents or turn slots; Send Spy and Spy on Relations stay open to it
  * The six local covert effect operations are queued when chosen and resolved
    at daily maintenance, as in the original; the fee, the agent and the
    per-turn slot are taken at the menu and the result lands on the sender's
    next report. Send Spy, Spy on Relations and Expose Enemy Ops still answer
    at once
  * Every local covert operation is resolved by the original's own roll. Neither
    realm's agent count changes the odds; each operation carries its own
    difficulty, so the simpler ones land 55 percent of the time, Demoralize
    Forces and Bomb Enemy Targets 40, and Bribery 32.5
  * A covert operation may be run once per turn each, rather than one operation
    of any type per turn. Send Spy, Spy on Relations and Expose Enemy Ops have
    no limit
  * A bribed agent doubles the briber's own odds against that realm. It used to
    make that realm's operations against the briber fail
  * Expose Enemy Ops shields against one realm — one the caller already holds a
    bribed agent inside, chosen from a list of those — for a day, and turns away
    nine of that realm's operations in ten. It used to block every realm's
    operations outright. It costs no agent and takes no per-turn slot
  * Bomb Enemy Targets is one covert operation, as in the original, instead of a
    submenu of seven lettered variants. A successful strike destroys part of one
    of the target's holdings, picked at random from its people, troopers,
    agents, tanks, jets and food store, and needs no Bombers. The seven variants
    remain on the InterPlanetary Special Operations menu, where the original
    keeps them
  * Stir Revolts and Demoralize Forces subtract points from popular support and
    military morale rather than scaling them, and hold both at 5. The scaling
    figures came from the resolver for operations arriving in a packet, which
    uses a different operation table
  * Support Dissensions sends 1 to 19 percent of the target's troopers fleeing,
    averaging a tenth, in place of a flat tenth
  * Set Up voids every treaty between the two realms, not only a Full Defense
    Alliance, and the attempt is rolled against each of them
  * Bribery is refused before any gold is charged when a bribed agent is already
    inside that realm
  * The R5-Slappenheimer Handling setting gates the InterPlanetary
    R5-Slappenheimer, which is the only menu that now fires one
  * A pending trade deal and treaty offer are now answered before the "since
    your last play" entries rather than after them, and the trade deal comes
    first, matching the original
  * Choosing the treaty a realm already holds with you now reports that the
    agreement stands, instead of offering to break it
  * Added barons-ftn, an optional helper that hands outbound packets to an FTN
    mailer as Type-2 file-attach netmail
  * A Full Defense Alliance partner is told what it lost defending an ally, who
    it was defending, and who attacked. Those losses used to happen silently. A
    Technology Agreement partner is told about the battle too, as in the
    original, though it sends nothing and loses nothing
  * A Full Defense Alliance no longer lends agents. The Alliance Strength
    screen's agents column comes from a Terrorist Prevention pact, as in the
    original
  * A foiled covert operation now names the realm that sent the agent, as the
    original does; an operation that succeeds still names nobody
  * A market sale is no longer posted to the planet news. The seller is still
    told who bought what and for how much
  * The Diplomacy Modification screen no longer says planetary diplomacy is
    enforced by nothing: barons may only trade at a planet marked Allied
  * A realm under new realm protection can no longer trade. The Trading Market,
    trade deals and interplanetary bids all refuse it, as attacks already did
  * Sending a trade deal needs a treaty with the other realm, as in the original
  * -gen-board-key prints the key alone on its line. It used to print the board
    name in front of it, and a roster entry pasted whole left that board's
    packets unchecked instead of reporting an error
  * A realm's letter on the attack and covert target lists is its own seat.
    Allying with the realms above it used to re-letter everything below, so the
    key that struck one realm struck a different one next time
  * The InterPlanetary Special Operations menu works, where seven of its eight
    items did nothing before. Bomb Food Market, Bomb Trading Market, Bomb Trade
    Routes and Undermine Investments are aimed at another planet and wreck what
    it shares; Nuclear Assault, Chemical Bombing and R5-Slappenheimer name a
    baron there. All of them resolve on that planet's own board and report back
  * The Coordinator menu matches the original: Modify Diplomacy, Global Recon
    Request, View Diplomacy and Player List, keyed 1 to 4
  * Global Recon Request asks every other planet for a report on every realm it
    holds, and the answers land in the Spy Database
  * Bomb Trade Routes wrecks the goods riding in the target's pending trade
    deals rather than severing a standing treaty: most strikes come to nothing,
    some deals slip through, and a deal the bombers reach keeps 5-9% of every
    good. A deal is spared when its own two parties hold Protective Trade,
    whoever fired, so the pact no longer refuses the strike or shields a market
  * The End of Turn Statistics screen is bracketed by the original's blue inset
    rule, above and below, where it had none
  * The Empire Status block is bracketed by the original's blue inset rule,
    above the realm name and below the reign line, where it had none
  * The Specialization box is 14 columns wide, as in the original, with its
    title overhanging it
  * A Daily Bulletin change is drawn as in the original: bright cyan whichever
    way the day went, with only a rise carrying a green plus. The falling rows
    were red, which was hard to read against black

2026-08-15 (v0.0.5)
  * "?" at a planet prompt shows the list on the keypress; it no longer waits
    for Enter as well
  * The door attaches to the caller's socket when a Unix BBS gives it no live
    standard input. A native Synchronet door on Linux is handed the socket and a
    standard input connected to nothing, so the caller was dropped the moment the
    door started. Windows keeps its own rule, a drop file naming a socket
  * The door log records which I/O path was actually taken, not only what the
    drop file asked for
  * config-during-reset: a new Allow IP Allies to Trade at Market setting
    (enabled) opens interplanetary trading
  * Region Cost Change is a surcharge on big realms, as in the original: it
    does nothing under 300 regions and then adds to the per-region climb, where
    it used to scale the price of every region for everyone (#56)
  * Maintenance Costs scales upkeep at the original's own rates — a quarter at
    Low and four times at High, where it was half and double (#56)
  * The Trade Deal Costs setting scales what sending a trade deal charges — at
    the original's own rates, a sixth at Low and triple at High; it was stored,
    shown and broadcast while changing nothing (#56)
  * The "Paused" bar no longer runs into the screen drawn after it
  * config-during-reset: a new Money Cap setting sets how much gold a realm may
    hold, on hand and again in the bank — two billion (the original's own limit)
    up to 999 billion
  * Diplomacy is on the opening menu as well as the System menu, and Game Setup
    is on the System menu as well as the opening menu
  * config-during-reset: a new Pirates setting (enabled) takes the pirates out
    of the game
  * "-reset" honours "-no-ansi", "-ascii" and "-cp437"
  * A regular attack is fought out round by round: the side that breaks off
    loses the share of its forces it will accept, and the winner loses only what
    the strength ratio cost it, so a rout is cheap and an even fight is not
  * Attack Rewards and Attack Damage follow the original's own tables — High
    takes a quarter of the defender's regions and costs a third of the force,
    and None still costs a little
  * A battle report leads with what the fight cost both sides and closes with
    the result
  * Population follows the original's model: the kind of land a realm owns —
    Urban far outweighing every other type — scaled by popular support and
    divided by the tax rate. Acreage alone no longer houses anyone
  * Each turn a realm closes part of the gap between the people it has and the
    people its land can hold; they leave faster than they arrive, and faster
    still the harder it taxes. Above a 50% rate the turn's movement is docked a
    further quarter, whichever way it was going
  * Computer-run realms keep buying land instead of stopping for good once their
    upkeep catches up with their income; land and army now draw on one surplus
  * A computer-run realm lists no military on the Trading Market where the sysop
    has set Buy Military to anything but Yes: with the shop closed the market is
    the planet's only supply, and stocking it would hand every enemy the units
    the setting withholds
  * Food is two obligations: the people and the armed forces are billed apart
    and asked about apart, as in the original
  * Every military unit eats — one food per 200 troopers and per 10,000 of every
    other type, including units listed for sale
  * River fishing follows the original's yield: a base of 110 plus a draw,
    raised by technology; rivers take a quarter of their output as food and pay
    the rest as gold
  * Military morale is bought back the way popular support is: the request is
    priced off the army's size, and paying part of it buys part of the points
  * Military morale rises only by that boost — it no longer drifts back on its own
  * Troopers, jets and tanks desert at a rate drawn from a band chosen by morale,
    and stop deserting at 40; turrets, bombers and carriers never desert
  * Underpaying the armed forces costs military morale and underpaying land
    upkeep costs popular support, at the original's rates, and both land at
    rollover rather than mid-turn
  * A food shortfall bills the people's need against popular support and the
    army's against military morale, and drives nobody out of the realm
  * Added civil war: a realm that feeds its people under two thirds of their
    need, or pays under 90% of its land upkeep, loses half its popular support
    and a share of its land and of every kind of unit it owns
  * Auto-Pay Maintenance stays silent only while popular support and military
    morale both read 100
  * Declaring war costs a quarter of both popular support and military morale;
    attacking a partner outright breaks the pact and costs nothing, as in the
    original
  * Stir Revolts and Demoralize Forces scale their target's stat instead of
    docking a flat number of points; an AI's covert op cannot push either below 5
  * An unpopular realm reaches the planet news even in a turn without a riot
  * A nuclear strike ruins land rather than destroying it: the target keeps every
    region and its upkeep but earns nothing from the ruined share
  * A chemical strike ruins land into waste, gasses a fifth of the target's
    people and knocks down its military morale and popular support
  * A biological strike kills a share of the target's people and troopers and
    halves its military morale, leaving the land alone
  * Each missile is priced by its target — the nuclear by its land, the chemical
    by its people and land, the biological by its troopers, people and land —
    quoted once the target is chosen, and can be declined; one the gold in hand
    cannot cover is paid out of the bank
  * A nuclear, chemical or biological strike raises the attacker's score, and
    none of them can wipe out a realm
  * Waste is a region type of its own, listed on the region tables; it is
    decontaminated during maintenance and its owner shares the recovered land
    out across region types
  * Send Message addresses a list of realms: a letter marks or unmarks one, "Z"
    marks them all, and Enter closes the list and opens the editor
  * Diplomacy takes the same list: a treaty is offered to, and war declared on,
    every marked realm at once, and "?" there lists relations rather than scores
  * Message target letters are the realm's own, so a fallen realm or the sender
    leaves a gap; they match the letters a message records as its recipients
  * A message-editor line wraps at the ruler's 68th column, carrying the word in
    progress down, and backspace at the start of a line re-opens the one above
  * A message is removed from the inbox once it is replied to, like Delete
  * A reply asks which lines of the message to quote, and opens with them
  * Empire Status is one unboxed block in the original's layout instead of two
    boxed pages, listing only the fields the realm has something in
  * Empire Status shows the cumulative score the scores board ranks on, where it
    used to show net worth under that label
  * The Score a turn earns is the net worth a realm starts with, rounded, rather
    than a written-down 213; the same figure for a standard start, but it now
    follows the starting setup if that is retuned
  * Today's News counts the planet live, so a new board's first day no longer
    reads as empty
  * "Since your last play" recap entries color the realm name and treaty type
    bright cyan
  * See Scores, the attack target list and the View Treaties roster mark a baron
    who is on the board with "(O)" beside their name
  * A pirate raid takes about a thirtieth of one kind of holding, named on its
    own line at the end of the income report, and reports its outcome as the
    original does: one of five random headlines, the loot, and what it cost
  * Pirates start with no army and no land and can field only what they have
    stolen, so a group left alone grows and a drained one is harmless
  * Beating pirates recovers a third of their gold, regions, agents and troopers
    and a quarter of their jets, turrets and tanks, and costs the attacker
    casualties
  * Pirates raid larger realms more often, a turn can carry more than one raid,
    and military listed on the Trading Market is not hidden from them
  * Attack Pirates marks a faction that raided you since your last play with "->"
    beside its name
  * Combat reports, pirate raid results, planet news and "since your last play"
    events wrap between words; the terminal was splitting figures in half
  * A bank at the Money Cap pays its interest into gold in hand instead of
    losing it; destroyed gold is reported in the event log
  * One investment still locks away at most two billion; deposits and
    withdrawals have no size limit
  * The investment rate is quoted and carried to a tenth of a percent, as the
    original does, and stays between 3.5% and 10% a day; it was whole percents
    in a band reaching 25% a day, which paid ninefold on a ten-day term
  * Both Configuration Editors hold Interest Rate to 50-200 and Standard
    Investment Rate to 35-100, the original's own ranges; they accepted 0,
    which is a bank that pays nothing
  * The whole bank balance earns interest; savings above 1.6 billion used to
    earn none, on a figure that came from a player guide and is in neither of
    the original's binaries
  * A withdrawal takes only as much gold as will fit in hand, leaving the rest in
    the bank
  * Figures of a billion or more are shown as "1.0000B" rather than in full
  * Amounts may be typed with a "b" suffix, as they already could with "k"/"m"
  * The Bombing Ops, Missile Ops and Clingy Annihilator settings take their
    operations off the Attack, Covert and InterPlanetary menus when disabled,
    hotkeys included
  * Added Waste, Population, and Morale and Support help topics

  Inter-BBS (league play only):
  * Added the original's three sysop reports: "-lastpacket" writes when a packet
    from each board was last processed, "-bbsinfo" every board with the version
    it runs and when it was last heard from, and "-playerlist" every realm on
    every board (League Coordinator only)
  * Packets carry the sending board's game version, so a Coordinator can see
    which boards are too old to read the newer ones
  * config-during-reset: the League Coordinator may require a game version of
    every board. A board below it has its packets refused and is marked on the
    BBSINFO report; only that board is refused, not the whole league
  * The required version is kept in "ibversion.cfg" beside the roster, on every
    board, so a sysop can read and set it without opening the game
  * config-during-reset: a league may have a name, set by its Coordinator and
    shown to players on Game Setup. The League Number still does the routing
  * Added Trading to the InterPlanetary Operations menu: a baron may bid on the
    Trading Market of any planet the Coordinator has marked Allied
  * A planet's market travels with its scores, so an ally sees what is offered
  * A bid holds the gold when it is sent. The far planet fills it only if the
    listing is still there at the same price and the alliance still stands when
    the bid lands, pays its seller, and sends the goods back; otherwise the gold
    comes home, with the reason
  * A listing that shrank fills as far as it goes and refunds the rest
  * A bid whose answer never arrives is given up on and refunded, on the same
    timer that brings lost forces home
  * Bids Out lists what a baron has bid for and how much gold is held
  * Added an Allied Markets help topic
  * Individual interplanetary strikes choose an attack type — Normal Attack,
    Quick Strike, or Extended Battle — trading strength against the land taken
    and the losses both sides accept. A Quick Strike fights at 120% strength,
    the figure the original's code applies rather than the 110% its help
    advertises
  * Both planets now report an interplanetary strike: the defending planet
    reads what it took and cost, or that it was repelled, where it read nothing
    at all; and an individual strike names the realm that sent it on both
    boards, where it was anonymous. A group attack stays the planet's doing
  * A group attack asks whether to target one baron or the whole planet with a
    single key, before the roster is drawn, as the original does; the whole
    planet was a row in the numbered baron list
  * An individual strike takes twice the land a group attack of the same weight
    would, costs one gold per unit, and asks for confirmation
  * A group attack's departure is set in hours, from 12 to 120, and the force
    leaves at that hour rather than on a day boundary
  * A baron who sends an interplanetary strike gets a private report when it
    returns: the attack type, the target realm and planet, whether it won, was
    beaten, found no such realm or found it protected, what was lost and what
    came home by unit type, and what it destroyed
  * Land captured on another planet is held until its owner next plays, and the
    region-type picker then asks what to hold it as; a group attack's land is
    shared between the barons who committed to it
  * An interplanetary strike costs the defender units, not only regions
  * A returning report for a strike whose forces have already been given up as
    lost is discarded instead of raising a second army
  * The sysop's Attack Damage setting reaches interplanetary strikes: None makes
    them almost bloodless, Low halves the losses and High doubles them
  * The Attack Costs setting scales what an interplanetary strike charges, and
    the price is capped at 200,000,000 gold
  * A terrorist operation costs 64 gold per region the launcher owns, scaled by
    the Terrorism Costs setting, quoted before it is sent
  * Inter-BBS packets carry the sending board's signature, so a board whose key
    is in the league roster cannot be impersonated, and the roster's node number
    for each end, preferred over the board name for authentication, addressing
    and identifying the League Coordinator
  * Added "-gen-board-key" to create this board's packet-signing key
  * A reset advances this board's own generation marker, so a packet left over
    from the cleared game is discarded instead of applied to the new one
  * This board's own settings — name, league number, packet directories — moved
    out of config.json into "bbs.cfg", editable without the settings editor; an
    existing board migrates on its next save with nothing for the sysop to do
  * "-ibbs-reset -import-bbs-cfg" takes a board's name, incoming-files directory
    and league number from an original Barren Realms Elite BBS.CFG
  * A league can be arranged as a tree instead of every board linking to every
    other one: the Coordinator writes the routing into the node list, and a
    member board then links only to its uplink (#106)
  * On a board that forwards for others, a packet arriving for another board is
    passed on instead of being left in the inbound directory (#106)
  * A board that forwards for its neighbours can give each one its own outbound
    directory, with "OutboundDirs" in config.json
  * A board may route some traffic its own way with an "ibroute.cfg" file
  * Added "-league-routes", which prints where this board sends each planet's
    packets
  * Sysops: a League Number keeps two leagues apart when they share one inbound
    directory
  * The Diplomacy List carries a standing with each planet — Enemy, None, Peace
    or Allied — set by the BBS Coordinator on a new Diplomacy Modification
    screen; as in the original it binds nothing and is never transmitted
  * The standing appears beside every planet named on any interplanetary screen
  * Allied Planets writes to every planet the chart marks Allied
  * The Planetary Treaties chart matches the original's layout and colors
  * Implemented the SDI Program: its funding allowance, its upkeep, its screen
    and the strength its funding buys all match the original
  * SDI strength is its funding spread over the realm's land, so taking regions
    thins the shield that covers them
  * SDI answers only strikes from another planet: it blunts an arriving realm's
    jets and bombers and intercepts incoming missiles, leaving a neighbour's
    missiles and bombing runs and the Clingy Annihilator alone
  * A group attack is not blunted by its target's SDI, as in the original
  * Clingy Annihilator Ops answers to 9 on the InterPlanetary Operations menu,
    and the interplanetary Special Operations menu is numbered, both as in the
    original
  * The InterPlanetary Operations and Special Operations menus show the gold on
    hand
  * Implemented IP mail
  * Travel Times shows the measured round trip to each planet, in hours or days
  * Every screen asks for a planet by name or number, with "?" for the list
  * Added an Attack Types help topic
  * Sysops: "-ibbs-reset" starts a new game as a league board and a plain
    "-reset" a stand-alone one, which is no longer asked the league settings;
    adding "-board-id" sets a member board up in one command, without the
    settings editor
  * The league Coordinator now also sets the money cap, unlimited food and
    idle-realm removal for every board; each was left to the local sysop, so two
    planets in one season could play by different rules
  * config-during-reset: added the Local Attacks (enabled), Local Attack Scoring
    (disabled) and Dupe Checking (enabled) league settings, editable in both
    Configuration Editors, shown on Game Setup and broadcast to the league
  * With Local Attacks disabled, barons on one board cannot attack each other and
    the Attack menu keeps only the pirate and alliance entries
  * With Local Attack Scoring disabled, a battle between barons on one board
    moves neither side's score
  * With Dupe Checking enabled, a caller found playing on another board in the
    league is locked out until one of the realms goes. Scores packets carry a
    hash of the handle for this, never the handle itself
  * The Configuration Editor sets the inter-BBS inbound and outbound
    directories, which now sit inside the data directory; a reset creates them
    and warns about packets left from the cleared game

2026-08-05 (v0.0.4)
  * The web server has been removed (an SSH server may replace it)
  * 32-bit builds charged the wrong region maintenance above 235 regions, often
    a negative amount, and misspent a computer baron's gold above 43 million
  * Windows: console ANSI handling is enabled automatically, and the
    Configuration Editor falls back to plain text on the legacy console, so
    "-reset" no longer needs the "ForceV2" registry setting (#98)
  * config-during-reset: a day is 10 turns by default; a new realm gets 15
    turns of protection
  * Worlds saved before v0.0.3 load again, with their mail text preserved
  * "-reset" works on a world the game can no longer read
  * A door caller whose BBS reports no ANSI support is sent plain text (#101)
  * Added "-no-ansi" to send plain text on a terminal that could render it
  * Added "-ascii" for a terminal that reads neither CP437 nor UTF-8
  * The game can be installed with Homebrew on macOS and Linux
  * Sysops: "-gen-coord-key" and "-coord-key" set up the league coordinator
    key; "-league-reset DATE" starts a new season on every board (#65)
  * The league Coordinator sends the roster of boards to every board (#64)
  * A board in a league has no computer barons, and none can be added
  * League orders carry the coordinator's signature, and a board refuses any
    it cannot check (#53)
  * A packet that arrives twice is ignored (#53)
  * The Configuration Editor gained the interplanetary settings: per-day caps
    for group attacks, terrorist ops and bombing ops, attack and terrorism
    costs, switches for bombing, missile and Clingy Annihilator operations, and
    how long a strike waits for a result before its forces come home
  * The line-based Configuration Editor is split across four pages, moved
    through with N and P (#100)
  * Game Setup shows the whole ruleset
  * Game Setup shows the interplanetary rules on a league board
  * The Clingy Annihilator is built the way the original builds it: funded a
    million gold at a time, priced by the target planet's size, and launched
    or scrapped by the baron who started it (#16)
  * A planet is warned about a Clingy Annihilator aimed at it while it is being
    built and while it is in the air, and can send jets to intercept (#63)
  * Implemented Indiv. Attack Force: strike one named baron on another planet
    without waiting for a group to form (#62)
  * Spying on another planet queries that planet and returns real figures (#61)
  * Group attacks, terrorist ops and bombing ops have their own per-day limits
  * Forces sent to another board come home if no result arrives within the
    number of days the game settings allow (#96)
  * Implemented Queen Royale tax refund (#93)
  * A treaty proposer is told whether the offer was accepted or rejected
  * View Treaties lists every realm
  * View Treaties lists sent treaty offers that are still unanswered (#92)
  * A new treaty offer to the same realm replaces the one still waiting
  * Alliance Strength gained a Total Forces line
  * Send Spy succeeds just over half the time, whatever agents either side
    holds
  * Covert agents cost more the longer a realm has been playing
  * Unit prices move within the original's price ranges
  * Debt no longer lowers net worth
  * A dead realm is worth nothing on the scores screen
  * Lists that cannot show a moving highlight are numbered instead, and the
    highlighted row is marked with a character as well as a color (#99)
  * A failed action is marked with a character, so it is not told from a
    successful one by color alone
  * Messages longer than the screen break between words
  * The title screen fits a 24-row terminal, and its prompt sits directly
    below the art
  * New mail is shown at the start of a turn instead of being offered first
  * Enter is ignored at the message prompt
  * Message boxes match the original's size and show the send time
  * "Since your last play" items are numbered and timestamped
  * Treaty offers, View Treaties and Alliance Strength match the original's
    layout and colors
  * Maintenance paid under Auto-Pay is reported as one total, as the original
    reports it
  * The Daily Bulletin title omits the board name on a stand-alone board
  * The System menu lists Covert Operations
  * German and Russian said food had SPOILED where it was consumed

2026-07-31 (v0.0.3)
  * Updating from an earlier version requires a game reset (-reset)
  * Updating also requires running "immortal-barons -set-dropfile" once:
    the door now needs to know which drop file your BBS writes, and will
    not start until it is set
  * PCBOARD.SYS and DORINFO1.DEF drop files are now supported, along
    with DOOR32.SYS and DOOR.SYS
  * Computer barons play far better — they build a mix of region types,
    manufacture their own units, set their tax rate, propose treaties,
    run covert operations, and turn to defense when threatened (#69)
  * Land maintenance now costs what it does in the original — much more
    than before, so holding a large realm is a real expense; military
    maintenance is ten times cheaper, also matching the original
  * Land is no longer unlimited — each realm gets a daily allowance of
    new land it may buy ("Land Created / Day" in the game settings)
  * Technology regions now work as in the original: research builds up
    over many turns across fifteen areas, and selling the regions keeps
    what you have already learned
  * The Queen Royale now takes a tax from each turn's income; paying her
    short costs popular support (#52)
  * Popular support now follows the original's math: a tax rate under
    30% wins support back every turn, and riots cost more the harder you
    tax
  * The gold asked for to boost popular support now grows with your
    population
  * Two empires now hold one agreement at a time, as in the original: a
    new pact replaces the one before it (#88)
  * Declaration Of War ends an agreement with no cost at home; attacking
    a realm you have an agreement with breaks it and costs you popular
    support instead (#88)
  * An ally in a Full Defense Alliance now sends 30% of its mobile
    forces to help defend you
  * A Regular Attack captures more regions from a target whose net worth
    is spread thin over its land, and fewer from a dense, developed one
  * A tank is now worth three troopers with no Headquarters, rising to
    five with a finished one, matching the original — it used to be four
    rising to eight
  * A HeadQuarters costs more the longer your realm has been playing,
    matching the original — build one early
  * Only troopers eat food now, matching the original — jets and tanks
    were being charged for rations they never needed, so an armored
    realm was paying about 40% too much food
  * Rivers now produce gold and food every turn, instead of one or the
    other (#67)
  * Agricultural regions grow a little more food in a good year, instead
    of the same amount every turn, matching the original
  * Desert regions earn more, matching the original game
  * A player attacked while at the menus now sees a notice at their next
    keypress, and attack/sell orders trimmed by concurrent losses say so
  * A realm whose owner never played a turn is removed at the next
    maintenance, and so is any realm left unplayed for 10 days; you can
    always start a new realm afterwards (#83)
  * Maintenance now advances the game at most one day at a time, so the
    computer barons cannot race ahead while nobody is playing
  * Read Messages now shows one message at a time (Reply/Delete/Ignore/
    Quit), matching the original — Ignore keeps a message for next time,
    only Delete removes it
  * The date the game started now appears above the main menu
  * The Queen Royale now makes proclamations in the daily news
  * Sysops: "immortal-barons -spectate N" plays a computer-only game for
    N days and prints a per-day summary, for balance testing (#42)

2026-07-24 (v0.0.2)
  * Winning a Regular Attack now lets you choose the types of the
    regions you capture (#58)
  * Winning a pirate raid that seizes land lets you choose its region
    types too (#21)
  * Combat reports now break down casualties by unit type for both
    the attacker and the defender
  * Pirate raids now happen randomly across turns (about 1 in 5)
    instead of all at once each day (#21)
  * The army now eats far less food, matching the original game — a
    large standing army is no longer food-crippling
  * Renamed the pirate factions to original names
  * Screens follow BRE more closely — highlighted figures, the news
    masthead/bulletin layout, and region-table colors
  * "Since your last play" events now show after you start your turn,
    not before the main menu
  * Windows door: works when the BBS hands over a non-blocking socket
    (MagicBBS) (#37)

2026-07-22
  * v0.0.1 released
